 ### Algorithm: Z-Order (Morton) Encoding
-Z-order encoding interleaves the bits of the input coordinates. For 3D coordinates $(x, y, z)$ with $B$ bits each:
-
-1. Initialize $I = 0$.
-2. For bit position $b$ from $0$ to $B-1$:
-   - Extract the $b$-th bit of $x$: $x_b = (x \gg b) \& 1$.
-   - Extract the $b$-th bit of $y$: $y_b = (y \gg b) \& 1$.
-   - Extract the $b$-th bit of $z$: $z_b = (z \gg b) \& 1$.
-   - Set bits in $I$ at positions $3b, 3b+1, 3b+2$:
-     $$ I = I \ | \ (x_b \ll 3b) \ | \ (y_b \ll (3b+1)) \ | \ (z_b \ll (3b+2)) $$
-3. Return $I$.
+Z-order encoding interleaves the bits of the input coordinates [G]. For 3D coordinates $(x, y, z)$ with $B$ bits each:
+```python
+def z_order_encode(x, y, z, B):
+    I = 0
+    for b in range(B):
+        xb = (x >> b) & 1
+        yb = (y >> b) & 1
+        zb = (z >> b) & 1
+        I |= (xb << (3 * b)) | (yb << (3 * b + 1)) | (zb << (3 * b + 2))
+    return I
+```
+[G]
 
 ### Algorithm: Z-Order Decoding
-1. Initialize $x=0, y=0, z=0$.
-2. For bit position $b$ from $0$ to $B-1$:
-   - Extract bits from $I$:
-     $$ x_b = (I \gg 3b) \& 1 $$
-     $$ y_b = (I \gg (3b+1)) \& 1 $$
-     $$ z_b = (I \gg (3b+2)) \& 1 $$
-   - Set the $b$-th bit of each coordinate:
-     $$ x = x \ | \ (x_b \ll b) $$
-     $$ y = y \ | \ (y_b \ll b) $$
-     $$ z = z \ | \ (z_b \ll b) $$
-3. Return $(x, y, z)$.
+```python
+def z_order_decode(I, B):
+    x, y, z = 0, 0, 0
+    for b in range(B):
+        xb = (I >> (3 * b)) & 1
+        yb = (I >> (3 * b + 1)) & 1
+        zb = (I >> (3 * b + 2)) & 1
+        x |= (xb << b)
+        y |= (yb << b)
+        z |= (zb << b)
+    return x, y, z
+```
